 "interpretation": [
  {
   "heading": "1. Masking impossible actions is the largest gain so far",
   "body": "B2 7.60 vs honest map 5.87, same model and reward. Experiment A on its final checkpoint shows why: buttons that sat at exactly 0% in the states where they would work now get pressed there (furnace 3.2% over 599 states, placing stone 2.4%, placing a table 75%, the wood pickaxe 100%), and every no-effect press is now a basic action (DO, NOOP, a blocked move). Once a useless press can no longer teach the agent that a button is bad, it presses that button when it works."
  },
  {
   "heading": "2. Telling helps, masking helps more",
   "body": "B1 (flags as input) reaches 6.90: both wood crafts survive in one run for the first time and the furnace comes off zero, but placing stone stays at 0.0% across 452 chances and about 6% of presses still go to impossible buttons. The information helps; removing the suppressing gradient helps more. Masking changes the action space relative to published Craftax baselines, so it is reported as a separate arm."
  },
  {
   "heading": "3. The next wall is sequencing, not suppression",
   "body": "In B2 the stone pickaxe was craftable in only 5 of 5,167 evaluation steps: the agent mines stone but rarely returns to a table holding both wood and stone. That gates iron and every 3-5-point achievement after it. It is a planning problem, so the next experiment should target multi-step sequences (experiment D, return-then-explore, or a sub-goal signal), not the action distribution."
  },
  {
   "heading": "4. Survival is still unsolved",
   "body": "Lifespans stay at or below the random band, except B1 (311, mean pulled up by a few long episodes; median 265). B2 dies earliest (246) and half of its deaths are mobs or lava: the more it does, the more it is exposed."
  },
  {
   "heading": "5. What to run next",
   "body": "Second seeds of B2 and the honest map, since every comparison here is single-seed. Then a sequencing experiment for the stone pickaxe. Explain the WAKE_UP gap before claiming the map model helps."
  }
 ],
